<p>Buying a home is one of the most significant financial decisions most people will ever make. Whether you're a first-time buyer stepping onto the property ladder or a seasoned investor expanding your portfolio, the journey from viewing to completion is filled with potential traps that can cost thousands of pounds and cause months of unnecessary stress. The UK property market, with its distinct legal framework, stamp duty thresholds, and survey requirements, can feel overwhelming — particularly if you're approaching it without proper guidance.</p>

<p>At Ark Housing, we've spent years helping buyers, landlords, and tenants navigate the complexities of the property market. In this guide, we highlight the most common mistakes home buyers make and, more importantly, how to sidestep them entirely. Knowledge, patience, and the right team around you can make the difference between a smooth purchase and a costly lesson.</p>

<h2>1. Skipping the Property Survey</h2>

<p>One of the most widespread — and costly — mistakes buyers make is skipping or downgrading the property survey. It can be tempting to save a few hundred pounds by opting for a basic mortgage valuation rather than a full HomeBuyer's Report or Building Survey. However, a mortgage valuation is carried out for the lender's benefit, not yours. It won't reveal hidden defects such as subsidence, Japanese knotweed, structural cracks, or failing roofs.</p>

<p>A full Building Survey (formerly called a Structural Survey) typically costs between £500 and £1,500 depending on the property size, but it can save you tens of thousands of pounds by uncovering problems before you exchange contracts. If serious issues are found, you can renegotiate the purchase price, request the seller to fix the problem, or walk away entirely — with your deposit intact. Never let price sensitivity on a survey cost you far more down the line.</p>

