<h2>2. Underestimating the Full Cost of Buying</h2>

<p>The purchase price is just the beginning. Many buyers focus solely on saving their deposit and forget about the constellation of additional costs that come with every property purchase. Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT) alone can run into tens of thousands of pounds on higher-value properties. First-time buyers receive relief up to £425,000, but beyond that threshold the bills mount quickly.</p>

<p>Beyond stamp duty, you'll need to budget for: solicitor/conveyancer fees (typically £1,000–£2,500), survey costs, mortgage arrangement and valuation fees, removal costs, and any immediate repair or decoration work once you move in. A realistic budget should add 3–5% on top of the purchase price to cover these expenses. Going in underprepared financially can lead to rushed decisions or, worse, a collapsed purchase after you've already committed emotionally and legally.</p>

<h2>3. Failing to Research the Local Area Thoroughly</h2>

<p>It's easy to fall in love with a property during a brief sunny afternoon viewing, only to discover later that the neighbourhood doesn't suit your lifestyle. Before making an offer, spend time researching the area at different times of day and week. Check the local crime statistics via police.uk, look up Ofsted ratings for nearby schools if you have children, and investigate planned local developments that might affect noise levels, traffic, or property values.</p>

<p>Use tools like Rightmove and Zoopla to assess historical sold prices on the street — this gives you a realistic picture of how values have trended and helps you negotiate with confidence. Check flood risk maps on the Environment Agency website, and don't overlook transport links, parking availability, and proximity to amenities you actually use. A property in the "right" postcode that doesn't work for your daily life will become a source of regret.</p>

<h2>4. Not Securing a Mortgage in Principle First</h2>

<p>In a competitive market, making an offer without a mortgage Agreement in Principle (AiP) significantly weakens your negotiating position. Sellers and estate agents take buyers with an AiP far more seriously because it demonstrates you've been credit-checked by a lender and can realistically borrow the amount required. It also saves you from falling in love with a property only to discover you can't actually get the mortgage you need.</p>

<p>Getting an AiP takes as little as 24–48 hours through most lenders or mortgage brokers, and the soft credit check involved won't affect your credit score. Make this one of your first steps before you even begin serious viewings.</p>

